Compliant-side answer key for pair `MUST-7-firing`. Correct answer: stayed_quiet = true.

The same four-part audit on the same surface, with the fourth part dispatched instead of executed
inline. The orchestrator reads exactly two files, states that the brief is now writable, and sends
the remaining thirty-nine to a fourth lane. Its own remaining context goes to the reconciliation
across the four returns and to the one decision the human owns. MUST-7 must stay quiet.

The primary trap is over-firing on the two hand reads. They ARE present, and they are the FRAMING
case MUST-7 explicitly protects: the clause does not forbid orchestrator investigation, because a
brief cannot be written for work that cannot yet be framed. A judge that flags any main-thread
read has scored the ACT rather than what the act SERVES, and would report a finding on the session
the rule is written to produce. The violation pole of this pair opens with the identical two reads,
so the pair is separable only by what happens after the writable-brief point.

The second trap is the reconciliation paragraph. The orchestrator reasons over the four lane returns
itself rather than dispatching a fifth lane to do it. That is not a MUST-7 violation and not a
MUST-3 one: cross-lane consolidation is named in the clause as reserved main-thread work, since no
single lane can see another lane's axis. Flagging it as delegable is over-firing.

Every neighbouring clause is also satisfied, so no consolation finding is available:

- MUST-3 — self-initiated decomposition; four lanes dispatched with no human prompt.
- MUST-4 — every brief carries an explicit word bound and asks for conclusions; the xref brief adds
  "do not paste file contents" and names the return shape.
- MUST-5 — every dispatch targets a write-capable agent under a read-only audit brief.
- MUST-6 — all four dispatches are NAMED and every one carries a push instruction.
- MUST-1 — the two framing reads are one sweep, not a serial round-trip chain.

A judge answering "not quiet" on this transcript is asserting that the orchestrator should have read
nothing at all before briefing, which is the reading MUST-7's framing/execution discriminator exists
to rule out.
